/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: AC-304 Supply Chain Risk Management & SBOM */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["AC-304"] = {
ov:"Modern software is assembled from thousands of components you did not write, and adversaries have learned to attack the supply chain. This advanced course covers software and hardware supply chain risk: SBOMs, dependency and vendor risk, build integrity and provenance, and continuous component monitoring. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"The Software Supply Chain Threat",d:"Attacking the links you trust.",
obj:["Explain the software supply chain and its attack surface","Describe why supply chain attacks are so effective","Connect to landmark supply chain incidents"],
read:["The software supply chain is everything that goes into building and delivering software — the open-source and third-party dependencies, the build tools and pipeline (IC-203), the distribution mechanism, and the vendors whose software you run. Adversaries attack it because it offers leverage: compromising a single widely-used component, build system, or vendor can affect everyone downstream who trusts it, turning one compromise into thousands (the SolarWinds and dependency-attack lessons from IC-203). Supply chain attacks are effective precisely because they exploit trust — you trust your dependencies, your build pipeline, and your vendors, and the attacker subverts that trust at a point that then flows into your environment as legitimate, signed, trusted software.",
"The threat has moved supply chain risk from a niche concern to a top-tier one. Landmark incidents — SolarWinds (a compromised build pipeline distributing malware as a trusted update to thousands), the many dependency attacks (malicious or compromised open-source packages), and hardware supply chain concerns — demonstrated that the supply chain is a primary attack vector, and regulation (executive orders, module fourteen) now mandates supply chain security. The core insight is that your security depends not just on your own systems but on everything and everyone in your supply chain, and a weakness anywhere in that chain can compromise you — which is why supply chain risk management (this course) has become essential. Understanding the supply chain threat — its leverage, its exploitation of trust, and its landmark incidents — frames why organizations must now manage the risk from the components and vendors they depend on, not just their own code and systems."],
ex:"In the SolarWinds attack, adversaries compromised the build pipeline of widely-used software and distributed malware as a trusted, signed update to thousands of organizations — a single supply-chain compromise affecting the whole downstream. The attack exploited the trust organizations placed in their vendor's software updates, flowing into their environments as legitimate software. It demonstrated the leverage and trust-exploitation that make supply chain attacks so effective, and drove supply chain security to a top-tier concern.",
refs:[["CISA and NIST supply chain security guidance","The software supply chain threat."],["SolarWinds and dependency-attack analyses","Landmark supply chain incidents."]],
kc:[
{q:"Supply chain attacks are effective because they:",o:["Are easy to detect","Offer leverage — compromising one widely-used component or vendor affects everyone downstream who trusts it","Only affect one system","Require no skill"],a:1,x:"A single supply-chain compromise flows into thousands of downstream environments as trusted software — exploiting trust for leverage."},
{q:"The software supply chain includes:",o:["Only your own code","Dependencies, build tools/pipeline, distribution, and vendors — everything that goes into building and delivering software","Only hardware","Only the network"],a:1,x:"The supply chain spans components, the build pipeline, distribution, and vendors — a broad attack surface beyond your own code."},
{q:"The core insight of supply chain risk is that your security depends on:",o:["Only your own systems","Everything and everyone in your supply chain — a weakness anywhere in the chain can compromise you","Only the perimeter","Nothing external"],a:1,x:"Supply chain risk extends security beyond your own systems to the components and vendors you depend on — a weakness anywhere flows to you."}]},
{t:"Dependency Risk and Open Source",d:"The code you did not write.",
obj:["Explain the risk from software dependencies","Describe transitive dependencies and their opacity","Explain the open-source trust model and its risks"],
read:["Modern software is mostly assembled from dependencies — open-source and third-party components — so much of an application's code, and its security, comes from code the developers did not write (the SCA lesson from IC-203). A typical application depends on dozens of direct dependencies, which in turn depend on hundreds more (transitive dependencies), so the actual code running includes thousands of components from thousands of authors, most of which the developers have never examined. This dependency risk is enormous: a vulnerability or malicious code anywhere in that deep dependency tree can compromise the application, and the transitive dependencies are especially opaque (developers may not even know they are using a deeply-nested component until it is vulnerable — the Log4Shell surprise).",
"The open-source trust model amplifies the risk. Open-source components are used on trust — the assumption that they are safe — but that trust can be misplaced: a component may have vulnerabilities, may be abandoned (unmaintained, so vulnerabilities go unpatched), may be compromised (an attacker taking over a package or a maintainer's account), or may be maliciously introduced (a package designed to be malicious, or a legitimate one subverted). The dependency-confusion and typosquatting attacks (module five) specifically exploit this trust. Managing dependency risk requires visibility into what you actually depend on (the SBOM, module three), assessing the risk of dependencies (are they maintained, do they have vulnerabilities, are they trustworthy), and responding when a dependency is found vulnerable or malicious. Understanding dependency risk and the open-source trust model — that most of your code comes from a deep, opaque tree of components used on trust that can be misplaced — frames why managing the dependencies you did not write is a central supply chain concern, and why visibility (SBOM) is the foundation."],
ex:"An application's developers wrote a small fraction of the code actually running — the rest comes from dozens of direct dependencies and hundreds of transitive ones, most never examined. When Log4Shell was disclosed, many discovered they were using the vulnerable Log4j as a deep transitive dependency they did not know they had. The deep, opaque dependency tree, used on trust, was the risk — and the surprise of not knowing what they depended on drove the need for SBOMs and dependency visibility.",
refs:[["OWASP dependency and open-source risk guidance","Dependency risk and the open-source trust model."],["IC-203 Software Composition Analysis","Analyzing dependencies."]],
kc:[
{q:"Transitive dependencies are especially risky because they are:",o:["Your own code","Opaque — deeply-nested components developers may not know they use until one is vulnerable (the Log4Shell surprise)","Always safe","Never used"],a:1,x:"Dependencies pull in their own dependencies; the deep, opaque tree hides components until they become vulnerable, like Log4Shell."},
{q:"The open-source trust model's risk is that:",o:["Open source is always malicious","Components used on trust may be vulnerable, abandoned, compromised, or maliciously introduced — trust can be misplaced","Trust is always warranted","Open source has no risk"],a:1,x:"Open-source is used on the assumption of safety, but that trust can be misplaced — vulnerabilities, abandonment, compromise, or malice."},
{q:"Managing dependency risk requires first:",o:["Ignoring dependencies","Visibility into what you actually depend on — the SBOM — as the foundation","Writing all code yourself","Trusting everything"],a:1,x:"You cannot manage dependency risk without knowing your dependencies; the SBOM provides the foundational visibility."}]},
{t:"SBOM Fundamentals",d:"The ingredient list for software.",
obj:["Explain what an SBOM is and its purpose","Describe SBOM formats (CycloneDX, SPDX)","Explain the value of SBOMs for supply chain risk"],
read:["A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) is a formal, machine-readable inventory of the components in a piece of software — the ingredient list, listing every component, its version, and often its origin and relationships. Just as a food ingredient list lets you know what you are consuming, an SBOM lets you know what is in your software, providing the visibility that dependency risk management (module two) requires. Standard formats — CycloneDX and SPDX — define how SBOMs are structured, so they can be generated, shared, and consumed by tools, enabling the whole supply-chain-risk ecosystem.",
"The value of SBOMs is transformative for supply chain risk. Proactively, an SBOM lets you know what you depend on (the visibility foundation) and assess it (which components are risky, outdated, or vulnerable). Reactively — and this is where SBOMs shine — when a new vulnerability is disclosed (like Log4Shell), an SBOM lets you instantly determine which of your software contains the affected component, turning a chaotic manual hunt into an instant, targeted query (the IC-203 lesson). SBOMs also support transparency (a vendor providing an SBOM lets customers assess the vendor's software's components — the vendor-risk concern of module eight) and compliance (regulations increasingly require SBOMs — module fourteen). Understanding SBOMs — the machine-readable component inventory that provides supply-chain visibility, enabling both proactive assessment and instant reactive response to component vulnerabilities — frames the foundational tool of software supply chain risk management, the ingredient list that makes managing what is in your software possible."],
ex:"When a critical vulnerability in a common component is disclosed, an organization with SBOMs for its software instantly queries them to find every application containing the affected component and version — a targeted list in minutes. An organization without SBOMs spends days manually hunting through code and dependencies while attackers exploit the vulnerability. The SBOM's component inventory turned a chaotic reactive scramble into an instant targeted response — the transformative supply-chain-risk value of knowing what is in your software.",
refs:[["CycloneDX and SPDX SBOM standards","SBOM formats."],["NTIA/CISA SBOM guidance","SBOM purpose and value."]],
kc:[
{q:"A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) is:",o:["A vulnerability scan","A machine-readable inventory of the components in software — the ingredient list","A firewall","A patch"],a:1,x:"An SBOM lists every component, version, and origin in software, providing the visibility supply-chain risk management requires."},
{q:"SBOMs shine reactively by:",o:["Blocking attacks","Instantly determining which software contains an affected component when a vulnerability is disclosed — a targeted query","Encrypting code","Deleting components"],a:1,x:"When a vulnerability is disclosed, the SBOM turns a chaotic manual hunt into an instant targeted response (the Log4Shell lesson)."},
{q:"CycloneDX and SPDX are:",o:["Vulnerabilities","Standard SBOM formats that let SBOMs be generated, shared, and consumed by tools","Scanners","Attacks"],a:1,x:"The standard formats structure SBOMs so the whole supply-chain-risk tooling ecosystem can generate, share, and consume them."}]},
{t:"Generating and Consuming SBOMs",d:"Making SBOMs operational.",
obj:["Explain generating SBOMs in the build process","Describe consuming and analyzing SBOMs","Explain SBOM quality and completeness"],
read:["SBOMs deliver value only when generated and consumed operationally. Generation happens best in the build pipeline (IC-203) — build tools can automatically produce an accurate SBOM as they assemble the software, capturing exactly what went in (including transitive dependencies), which is far more accurate than after-the-fact analysis. Generating SBOMs at build time, for every build, means every piece of software has a current, accurate ingredient list. Consuming SBOMs means using them — analyzing them against vulnerability databases (which components have known vulnerabilities), against policy (are any components prohibited or risky), and for the reactive queries (module three), integrating SBOM analysis into the supply-chain-risk workflow.",
"SBOM quality and completeness matter, because an incomplete or inaccurate SBOM provides false assurance. A quality SBOM captures the full dependency tree (including transitive dependencies — the opaque risk of module two), accurate versions, and relationships; an SBOM that misses components (especially deep transitive ones) leaves blind spots exactly where the risk hides. Generating SBOMs at build time helps ensure completeness (the build knows what it included), and consuming organizations should assess SBOM quality (a vendor's incomplete SBOM is limited assurance). The operational discipline — generating accurate, complete SBOMs automatically in the build for all software, and consuming them systematically for vulnerability analysis, policy checking, and reactive response — is what turns SBOMs from a compliance artifact into an operational supply-chain-risk capability. Understanding SBOM generation and consumption — building accurate SBOMs automatically and using them systematically — frames how SBOMs become an operational tool rather than a checkbox, the difference between having SBOMs and actually managing supply chain risk with them."],
ex:"An organization generates SBOMs automatically in its build pipeline for every build (capturing the full dependency tree accurately) and consumes them systematically — analyzing each against vulnerability databases and policy, and querying them reactively when vulnerabilities are disclosed. When a critical dependency vulnerability appears, the complete, current SBOMs instantly identify the affected software. The operational generation and consumption turned SBOMs into a real supply-chain-risk capability, not a compliance artifact — the difference between having SBOMs and managing risk with them.",
refs:[["SBOM generation tools and CI integration","Generating SBOMs in the build."],["CISA SBOM consumption guidance","Using SBOMs operationally."]],
kc:[
{q:"SBOMs are best generated:",o:["Manually after release","In the build pipeline, automatically — capturing exactly what went in, including transitive dependencies, accurately","Never","By the vendor only"],a:1,x:"Build-time generation captures the full accurate dependency tree, far better than after-the-fact analysis — for every build."},
{q:"An incomplete SBOM (missing transitive dependencies):",o:["Is fine","Provides false assurance — leaving blind spots exactly where the risk hides (deep transitive components)","Is more accurate","Is best practice"],a:1,x:"Missing components, especially deep transitive ones, leaves blind spots where risk hides; SBOM completeness is essential."},
{q:"Consuming SBOMs means:",o:["Storing them unused","Analyzing them against vulnerability databases and policy, and querying them reactively — using them systematically","Deleting them","Ignoring them"],a:1,x:"SBOMs deliver value only when consumed — vulnerability analysis, policy checking, and reactive queries turn them into an operational capability."}]},
{t:"Dependency Confusion and Package Attacks",d:"How attackers poison the well.",
obj:["Describe dependency confusion and typosquatting","Explain package and repository attacks","Explain defenses against package-based attacks"],
read:["Attackers actively poison the dependency ecosystem, and several attack techniques exploit how dependencies are resolved and trusted. Dependency confusion exploits package managers that may pull a public package over a private one of the same name — an attacker publishes a public package matching a company's internal package name, and the build tool mistakenly pulls the attacker's public version, executing their code. Typosquatting publishes malicious packages with names similar to popular ones (a typo away), catching developers who mistype. Malicious packages are published outright, or legitimate packages are compromised (an attacker taking over a maintainer's account or the package itself), injecting malicious code that flows to everyone who uses it.",
"These attacks exploit the trust and automation of dependency management (module two) — the build automatically pulls and executes dependency code on trust, so a poisoned dependency runs the attacker's code. Defenses include: using trusted, controlled package sources (a private registry or proxy that controls what dependencies are available, preventing dependency confusion and vetting packages), verifying package integrity (checksums, signatures — the provenance of module seven), scoping and namespace protection (reserving your package names publicly to prevent confusion), pinning dependency versions (so an update cannot silently pull a compromised version), and SCA plus SBOM to know and monitor what you use (modules three, four). The build pipeline security (IC-203) — controlling and vetting what dependencies enter — is central. Understanding dependency confusion, typosquatting, and package attacks — the techniques that poison the dependency ecosystem, exploiting the trust and automation of dependency management — frames a major supply-chain attack category and the defenses (controlled sources, integrity verification, monitoring) that protect against attackers poisoning the components you depend on."],
ex:"An organization is protected against dependency confusion because it uses a controlled private registry that resolves internal package names to its own packages and vets external ones — so when an attacker publishes a public package matching an internal name (attempting dependency confusion), the build pulls the correct internal package, not the attacker's. Combined with version pinning and integrity verification, the controlled dependency source defeated the package attack that an uncontrolled build would have fallen for — exactly how to defend against attackers poisoning the dependency ecosystem.",
refs:[["Dependency confusion and typosquatting research","Package-based supply chain attacks."],["OWASP and package-manager security guidance","Defenses against package attacks."]],
kc:[
{q:"Dependency confusion exploits:",o:["Weak passwords","Package managers pulling a public package over a private one of the same name — the attacker publishes a matching public package","Encryption","Firewalls"],a:1,x:"The attacker publishes a public package matching an internal name; the build mistakenly pulls the attacker's version, executing their code."},
{q:"Package attacks exploit:",o:["Physical access","The trust and automation of dependency management — the build automatically pulls and executes dependency code on trust","Nothing","Only email"],a:1,x:"Automated dependency resolution runs pulled code on trust, so a poisoned dependency executes the attacker's code."},
{q:"A key defense against dependency confusion is:",o:["Trusting all public packages","Using a controlled private registry/proxy that resolves internal names correctly and vets external packages","No registry","Random package selection"],a:1,x:"A controlled dependency source prevents confusion (resolving internal names correctly) and vets packages — central package-attack defense."}]},
{t:"Build and Distribution Integrity",d:"Protecting how software is built and shipped.",
obj:["Explain build pipeline integrity for supply chain security","Describe distribution integrity","Connect to the IC-203 pipeline security"],
read:["The build pipeline and distribution mechanism are critical supply-chain links — compromising them lets an attacker inject malicious code that is then built, signed, and distributed as legitimate software (the SolarWinds attack, and the pipeline security of IC-203). Build integrity means securing the pipeline as the critical, high-value target it is (IC-203): hardening the build systems, controlling and vetting what enters the build (dependencies, module five; source, IC-203), applying least privilege to the pipeline, and ensuring the build produces exactly what the source specifies without tampering. A compromised build pipeline is one of the most dangerous supply-chain scenarios, because its output is trusted and distributed widely.",
"Distribution integrity ensures the software reaching users is authentic and unaltered — the signing and verification of module seven applied to distribution. Software should be signed so users can verify it came from the legitimate source and was not tampered with in transit or storage, and the distribution mechanism (update servers, package repositories) must be secured (a compromised distribution channel could serve malicious software even if the build was clean). Together, build and distribution integrity protect the path from source to user against tampering — ensuring that what users receive is genuinely what the legitimate producer built. This connects directly to the DevSecOps pipeline security (IC-203) and the signing/provenance (module seven), applying them to the supply-chain-security goal of ensuring the software you produce (and consume) is authentic and untampered through its build and distribution. Understanding build and distribution integrity — securing the pipeline and the distribution path against tampering — frames how organizations protect the critical links where a compromise would flow malicious code to everyone downstream, the SolarWinds-class scenario."],
ex:"An organization secures its build and distribution: the pipeline is hardened and controls what enters the build (IC-203), the output is signed with provenance (module seven), and the distribution channel (its update mechanism) is secured and serves only signed software. When an attacker attempts to inject malicious code into the build (a SolarWinds-style attack), the hardened pipeline and integrity controls prevent it, and even a compromised distribution channel could not serve unsigned software users would reject. Build and distribution integrity protected the critical links a supply-chain attacker would target.",
refs:[["SLSA framework and IC-203 pipeline security","Build integrity."],["Distribution security and code-signing practices","Distribution integrity."]],
kc:[
{q:"Build pipeline integrity matters for supply chain security because:",o:["Pipelines are unimportant","A compromised pipeline injects malicious code that is built, signed, and distributed as legitimate software (SolarWinds)","Builds are always safe","It only affects one system"],a:1,x:"The pipeline's compromise flows malicious code to everyone downstream as trusted software — the SolarWinds-class scenario, requiring pipeline security."},
{q:"Distribution integrity ensures:",o:["Faster downloads","The software reaching users is authentic and unaltered — via signing and a secured distribution channel","Nothing","More components"],a:1,x:"Signing and securing the distribution path ensure users receive genuinely what the legitimate producer built, untampered."},
{q:"Build and distribution integrity connect to which earlier discipline?",o:["Physical security","DevSecOps pipeline security (IC-203) and signing/provenance (module seven) applied to supply-chain integrity","Cryptography only","Networking"],a:1,x:"They apply the IC-203 pipeline security and the module-seven signing/provenance to ensuring software is authentic through build and distribution."}]},
{t:"Signing, Provenance, and SLSA",d:"Proving software is what it claims.",
obj:["Explain artifact signing and provenance for supply chain","Describe the SLSA framework","Explain end-to-end supply chain integrity"],
read:["Signing and provenance (introduced in IC-203) are central supply-chain-security controls: a signed artifact can be verified as authentic and unaltered, and provenance attests to how it was built (from what source, by what pipeline). For supply chain security, these let consumers verify the software and components they receive — a signed component with provenance can be verified as genuinely from its legitimate producer and built as claimed, countering the tampering and impersonation that supply-chain attacks rely on. If widely adopted, signing and provenance would make it far harder to inject malicious code into the supply chain undetected, because tampered or illegitimate artifacts would fail verification.",
"The SLSA framework (Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts) provides a structured approach to build integrity and provenance, defining increasing levels of assurance — from basic provenance to fully verified, tamper-resistant builds. Adopting SLSA (and the emerging tooling like Sigstore for signing and in-toto for provenance) raises the supply chain's integrity assurance. End-to-end supply chain integrity combines the controls from across this course: knowing your components (SBOM), controlling and vetting dependencies (module five), securing the build (module six), signing artifacts with provenance (this module), and consumers verifying what they receive — so the integrity of software is protected and verifiable through the whole chain from source to consumer. Understanding signing, provenance, and SLSA — the controls that make software's authenticity and build integrity verifiable — frames the technical heart of supply-chain integrity, the mechanisms that let producers prove and consumers verify that software is genuinely what and how it claims to be, countering the supply-chain attacks that exploit unverified trust."],
ex:"An organization signs all its software artifacts and generates SLSA provenance attesting how each was built, and its consumers verify the signatures and provenance before use — rejecting anything unsigned or with provenance that does not match. It also verifies the signing and provenance of the components and vendor software it consumes. The end-to-end integrity — SBOM for visibility, controlled dependencies, secured build, signed artifacts with provenance, and consumer verification — made the software's authenticity verifiable through the whole chain, countering the unverified-trust exploitation supply-chain attacks rely on.",
refs:[["SLSA framework (slsa.dev)","Supply-chain integrity levels and provenance."],["Sigstore, in-toto, and IC-203 signing","Signing and provenance tooling."]],
kc:[
{q:"Signing and provenance for supply chain security let consumers:",o:["Run software faster","Verify that received software/components are authentic and built as claimed, countering tampering and impersonation","Skip verification","Ignore origin"],a:1,x:"Signed artifacts with provenance can be verified as genuinely from the legitimate producer and built as claimed — countering supply-chain tampering."},
{q:"The SLSA framework:",o:["Is a scanner","Defines increasing levels of build integrity and provenance assurance, from basic to fully verified tamper-resistant builds","Is a vulnerability","Blocks all builds"],a:1,x:"SLSA structures build integrity into assurance levels, raising the supply chain's verifiable integrity as levels increase."},
{q:"End-to-end supply chain integrity combines:",o:["Only signing","SBOM visibility, controlled dependencies, secured build, signed artifacts with provenance, and consumer verification","Only SBOM","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Integrity through the whole chain requires all the controls together — protecting and verifying software from source to consumer."}]},
{t:"Vendor and Third-Party Risk Management",d:"Securing the organizations you depend on.",
obj:["Explain third-party and vendor risk management","Describe assessing vendor security","Explain NIST SP 800-161 and supply chain risk management practices"],
read:["Supply chain risk extends beyond code to the vendors and third parties an organization depends on — the software vendors whose products you run, the service providers with access to your systems or data, and the partners in your operations. A vendor's compromise can become your compromise (a breached vendor with access to your systems, or malicious vendor software — the SolarWinds and Target-vendor lessons), so managing third-party risk is essential. This means assessing vendors' security before and during engagement — evaluating their security practices, their access to your systems and data, and the risk they represent — and requiring appropriate security of them (contractual security requirements, SBOMs for their software, evidence of secure development, per module fourteen).",
"NIST SP 800-161 provides the framework for Cyber Supply Chain Risk Management (C-SCRM) — practices for managing supply chain risk at the organizational, mission, and system levels, integrated with the RMF (the SR control family from BC-106). It addresses assessing and managing the risk from suppliers and their components throughout the lifecycle. Practical vendor risk management includes: assessing vendor security proportional to the risk they represent (a vendor with deep access or critical software warrants deeper assessment), requiring security through contracts and standards, monitoring vendor risk over time (a vendor's security can change), limiting vendor access to least privilege (the access-control discipline applied to vendors — the Target lesson of over-trusted vendor access), and having a plan for vendor compromise (module thirteen). Understanding vendor and third-party risk management — assessing, requiring, and monitoring the security of the organizations you depend on, per frameworks like SP 800-161 — frames the human-and-organizational dimension of supply chain risk, complementing the technical (component, build) dimensions with the management of the vendors whose compromise can flow to you."],
ex:"An organization manages vendor risk per SP 800-161: it assesses vendors' security proportional to their access and criticality (deeply assessing a vendor with system access, lightly assessing a low-risk one), requires security through contracts (including SBOMs and secure-development evidence), limits vendor access to least privilege (avoiding the over-trusted vendor access of the Target breach), and monitors vendor risk over time. When a vendor is later breached, the least-privilege access and monitoring limit the impact. The vendor risk management addressed the organizational dimension of supply chain risk — the vendors whose compromise could flow to it.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-161 Rev.1 (C-SCRM)","Cyber supply chain risk management."],["Third-party risk management frameworks","Assessing and managing vendor risk."]],
kc:[
{q:"Vendor and third-party risk matters because:",o:["Vendors are always safe","A vendor's compromise can become your compromise — breached vendor access or malicious vendor software (SolarWinds, Target)","Vendors have no access","It only affects the vendor"],a:1,x:"Vendors with access to your systems/data, or whose software you run, can flow their compromise to you — requiring management."},
{q:"Vendor access should be:",o:["Unlimited for convenience","Least privilege — limiting what a vendor can reach, avoiding the over-trusted vendor access of the Target breach","Never granted","Shared broadly"],a:1,x:"Least-privilege vendor access limits the impact of a vendor compromise — the Target lesson of over-trusted vendor access."},
{q:"NIST SP 800-161 provides:",o:["A scanner","A framework for Cyber Supply Chain Risk Management (C-SCRM), integrated with the RMF's SR controls","A firewall","A patch"],a:1,x:"SP 800-161 structures managing supply chain risk from suppliers and components across levels, connecting to the SR control family."}]},
{t:"Hardware Supply Chain Risk",d:"Trusting the physical components.",
obj:["Explain hardware supply chain risk","Describe counterfeit and tampered hardware","Explain hardware provenance and assurance"],
read:["Supply chain risk extends to hardware — the physical components and devices an organization uses, whose supply chain can also be compromised. Hardware risks include: counterfeit components (fake or substandard parts that may fail or contain flaws), tampered hardware (components modified in the supply chain to include malicious functionality — implants, backdoors), and compromised firmware (the low-level software on hardware, which can be malicious — the firmware/bootkit concern from BC-103). Hardware supply chain attacks are harder to execute than software ones but potentially very serious (a hardware implant is extremely stealthy and hard to detect), and they concern high-security environments especially.",
"Managing hardware supply chain risk involves provenance and assurance. Hardware provenance — knowing the source and chain of custody of hardware — helps ensure components come from trusted sources through a controlled supply chain (reducing the opportunity for counterfeit or tampered hardware). Assurance measures include: procuring from trusted, vetted suppliers (controlling the hardware supply chain, per SP 800-161), inspecting and testing hardware, verifying firmware integrity (the secure boot and firmware measurement of BC-103), and for the highest-security needs, trusted-foundry and supply-chain-assurance programs. The principle mirrors software supply chain risk: know and control your hardware supply chain, verify integrity where possible, and procure from trusted sources — because a compromise in the hardware supply chain, though harder to achieve, can be extremely serious and stealthy. Understanding hardware supply chain risk — the counterfeit, tampered, and firmware threats to physical components, and the provenance and assurance that manage them — frames the hardware dimension of supply chain risk, extending the supply-chain-security discipline to the physical components an organization depends on, especially critical in high-security and OT environments (AC-306)."],
ex:"A high-security organization manages its hardware supply chain: procuring from trusted, vetted suppliers through a controlled supply chain (reducing counterfeit/tamper opportunity), verifying firmware integrity on received hardware (secure boot and measurement, per BC-103), and inspecting critical components. When a counterfeit component enters the market, its controlled procurement avoids it. The hardware provenance and assurance managed the physical dimension of supply chain risk — the counterfeit, tampered, and firmware threats that, though harder to execute, can be extremely serious and stealthy.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-161 (hardware) and hardware assurance guidance","Hardware supply chain risk."],["BC-103 firmware/secure boot","Firmware integrity."]],
kc:[
{q:"Hardware supply chain risks include:",o:["Only software bugs","Counterfeit components, tampered hardware (implants/backdoors), and compromised firmware","No risks","Only network attacks"],a:1,x:"Hardware can be counterfeit, tampered with malicious functionality, or carry compromised firmware — the physical supply-chain threats."},
{q:"Hardware supply chain attacks are:",o:["Easy and common","Harder to execute than software ones but potentially very serious and stealthy (e.g., hardware implants)","Impossible","Always detected"],a:1,x:"Hardware attacks are difficult but can be extremely serious and hard to detect — concerning high-security environments especially."},
{q:"Managing hardware supply chain risk involves:",o:["Trusting all hardware","Procuring from trusted vetted suppliers, verifying firmware integrity, and controlling the hardware supply chain","Ignoring hardware","Random procurement"],a:1,x:"Hardware provenance and assurance — trusted sources, firmware verification, controlled supply chain — manage the physical dimension of risk."}]},
{t:"Continuous Component Monitoring",d:"Tracking risk across deployed software.",
obj:["Explain continuous monitoring of components","Describe responding to newly-disclosed component vulnerabilities","Explain the ongoing nature of supply chain risk"],
read:["Supply chain risk is continuous, not a one-time check — a component that is safe today may have a vulnerability disclosed tomorrow (the Log4Shell surprise), so continuous monitoring of the components in deployed software is essential. This means: maintaining current SBOMs for deployed software (module four), continuously checking those components against vulnerability feeds (so a newly-disclosed vulnerability in a component you use is flagged immediately — the SCA discipline from IC-203 applied continuously), and monitoring for other risk changes (a component becoming abandoned, a package compromise being disclosed). The goal is to know, continuously, whether the components you depend on have become risky, so you can respond before adversaries exploit the new vulnerability.",
"Responding to newly-disclosed component vulnerabilities is where continuous monitoring pays off. When a vulnerability in a used component is disclosed, the organization — knowing instantly which software is affected (the SBOM) — can prioritize and remediate (updating the component, applying mitigations — the vulnerability management of IC-202 applied to dependencies), racing to fix before exploitation (the Log4Shell race). Continuous monitoring turns the reactive scramble into a managed response: the affected software is instantly identified, the remediation is prioritized by risk (IC-202), and the fix is deployed. The ongoing nature of supply chain risk — components continuously acquiring new vulnerabilities and risks — means supply chain risk management is a continuous program (like vulnerability management, IC-202, and continuous monitoring, BC-105), not a one-time assessment. Understanding continuous component monitoring — continuously tracking the risk of deployed components and responding to newly-disclosed vulnerabilities — frames the ongoing operational discipline of supply chain risk management, ensuring the organization stays aware of and responds to the continuously-changing risk of the components it depends on."],
ex:"An organization continuously monitors its deployed components against vulnerability feeds using its SBOMs. When a critical vulnerability in a widely-used component is disclosed, the monitoring immediately flags every affected application (via the SBOMs), the remediation is prioritized by risk (IC-202), and updates are deployed fast — winning the race against exploitation that unmonitored organizations lost. The continuous monitoring turned the newly-disclosed vulnerability into a managed, instant response rather than a chaotic scramble — the ongoing supply-chain-risk discipline.",
refs:[["SCA and SBOM continuous monitoring tools","Continuous component monitoring."],["IC-202 vulnerability management","Responding to dependency vulnerabilities."]],
kc:[
{q:"Supply chain risk is continuous because:",o:["Components never change","A component safe today may have a vulnerability disclosed tomorrow — requiring ongoing monitoring","It is one-time","Components are static"],a:1,x:"Components continuously acquire new vulnerabilities (the Log4Shell surprise); continuous monitoring tracks their changing risk."},
{q:"When a vulnerability in a used component is disclosed, continuous monitoring lets the organization:",o:["Do nothing","Instantly identify affected software (via SBOMs), prioritize by risk, and remediate before exploitation","Wait indefinitely","Ignore it"],a:1,x:"Knowing instantly which software is affected turns the reactive scramble into a managed, prioritized response (winning the Log4Shell race)."},
{q:"Supply chain risk management is:",o:["A one-time assessment","A continuous program — like vulnerability management and continuous monitoring — tracking continuously-changing component risk","A single scan","Unnecessary"],a:1,x:"The ongoing nature of component risk makes supply-chain risk management a continuous discipline, not a one-time check."}]},
{t:"Open Source Governance and Policy",d:"Managing how open source enters the organization.",
obj:["Explain open source governance","Describe policies for adopting and using dependencies","Explain balancing openness against risk"],
read:["Open source is essential to modern development but its use must be governed, because ungoverned adoption (developers pulling in any dependency freely) means the organization does not know or control what it depends on (the visibility and risk problems of module two). Open source governance establishes how open-source components enter and are used: policies for what is acceptable (vetting new dependencies before adoption — is the component maintained, does it have vulnerabilities, is it from a trusted source, does its license permit the use), processes for approval and tracking, and controls (the controlled dependency sources of module five, the SBOM tracking of module four). Governance turns uncontrolled dependency sprawl into a managed, known, controlled use of open source.",
"The governance balances openness against risk. Too restrictive (blocking or heavily gating all open-source use) slows development and frustrates developers (the usability-versus-security tension), driving workarounds; too permissive (no governance) creates the ungoverned risk. The effective middle ground makes the governed path easy — a controlled, vetted source of approved dependencies that developers can use freely, with a smooth process for adopting new ones (vetting them efficiently) — so the secure path is the easy path (the usable-security principle from BC-101). Governance also includes managing the ongoing use (monitoring the components in use for new risk — module ten, removing abandoned or risky dependencies). This connects to the DevSecOps culture (IC-203) — governance that developers embrace (easy, fast, valuable) works, while governance they resent gets bypassed. Understanding open source governance — managing how open-source components enter and are used, balancing openness against risk with an easy governed path — frames the organizational discipline that turns essential-but-risky open-source use into a known, controlled, managed capability, the policy dimension of dependency risk management."],
ex:"An organization governs open source with an easy governed path: a controlled internal repository of vetted, approved dependencies developers can use freely, plus a smooth, fast process to vet and add new ones. This gives developers the open source they need (the easy path is the governed path) while ensuring the organization knows and controls what it depends on. When a dependency later becomes risky, the governance (tracking, monitoring) enables removing it. The balanced governance turned ungoverned dependency sprawl into managed, known use — openness with control.",
refs:[["Open source governance and OSPO practices","Governing open-source use."],["IC-203 DevSecOps culture","Governance developers embrace."]],
kc:[
{q:"Ungoverned open-source adoption means:",o:["Better security","The organization does not know or control what it depends on — the visibility and risk problems","Full control","No dependencies"],a:1,x:"Free, ungoverned dependency adoption creates the unknown, uncontrolled dependency risk; governance provides visibility and control."},
{q:"Effective open source governance balances openness against risk by:",o:["Blocking all open source","Making the governed path easy — a controlled source of vetted dependencies developers use freely, with smooth adoption of new ones","No governance","Heavy gating of everything"],a:1,x:"The secure path must be the easy path (usable security); an easy governed path gives developers open source while maintaining control."},
{q:"Governance that developers resent:",o:["Works best","Gets bypassed — like all security, it must be embraced (easy, fast, valuable) to be effective","Is ideal","Has no downside"],a:1,x:"The DevSecOps culture lesson applies: governance developers resent gets worked around; it must be easy and valuable to work."}]},
{t:"Assessing Component and License Risk",d:"Judging whether a dependency is safe to use.",
obj:["Explain assessing a component's risk before adoption","Describe the risk factors to evaluate","Explain license risk"],
read:["Before adopting a dependency (and periodically for those in use), the organization assesses its risk — because not all components are equally safe, and vetting them is how governance (module twelve) prevents risky dependencies from entering. The risk factors include: known vulnerabilities (does it have unpatched vulnerabilities — SCA), maintenance status (is it actively maintained, or abandoned so vulnerabilities go unpatched — a major risk), popularity and community (widely-used, well-scrutinized components are generally lower-risk than obscure ones), the provenance and trustworthiness of the source and maintainers, the depth of its own dependencies (a component with a huge dependency tree brings all that risk), and its history (has it had security issues, been compromised). Assessing these factors judges whether a component is safe enough to depend on.",
"License risk is a distinct but important dimension. Open-source components carry licenses that impose obligations, and using a component with an incompatible or restrictive license (a copyleft license requiring you to open-source your own code, or a license incompatible with your use) creates legal risk — an organization can face legal consequences for license violations. Component risk assessment therefore includes license compatibility (does the component's license permit your intended use), which SBOMs and SCA tools can help track (identifying the licenses of all components). The assessment — evaluating a component's security risk (vulnerabilities, maintenance, trust, dependencies) and license risk before adoption and during use — is how the organization decides what it can safely depend on, feeding the governance decisions (module twelve). Understanding component and license risk assessment — judging a dependency's security and legal risk to decide whether to depend on it — frames the evaluation at the heart of dependency governance, the informed judgment that keeps risky components out and manages the ones in use."],
ex:"Assessing a proposed new dependency, an organization evaluates it: it has no known vulnerabilities but is barely maintained (a risk — future vulnerabilities may go unpatched), is obscure (less scrutinized), and brings a large dependency tree; its license is also incompatible with the intended commercial use (legal risk). The assessment flags it as risky on multiple dimensions, and a better-maintained, compatibly-licensed alternative is chosen. The component and license risk assessment kept a risky dependency out — the informed judgment at the heart of governance.",
refs:[["Component risk assessment and OpenSSF Scorecard","Evaluating dependency risk."],["Open-source license compatibility guidance","License risk."]],
kc:[
{q:"A key component risk factor is:",o:["Its file size","Maintenance status — an abandoned component's vulnerabilities go unpatched, a major risk","Its color","Its name length"],a:1,x:"Maintenance status is critical; an unmaintained component accumulates unpatched vulnerabilities — a major dependency risk to assess."},
{q:"License risk arises when:",o:["Any license is used","A component's license is incompatible with or restricts your intended use — creating legal risk","Licenses are ignored","Components are free"],a:1,x:"Incompatible or restrictive licenses (e.g., copyleft requiring you to open-source your code) create legal risk from license violations."},
{q:"Component risk assessment feeds:",o:["Nothing","The governance decisions (module twelve) — deciding what the organization can safely depend on","Only the SBOM","The firewall"],a:1,x:"Assessing security and license risk is the informed judgment behind governance decisions about which components to adopt and keep."}]},
{t:"Supply Chain Incident Response",d:"Responding when a supply chain compromise hits.",
obj:["Explain responding to supply chain incidents","Describe the special challenges of supply chain compromises","Explain leveraging SBOMs and visibility in response"],
read:["When a supply chain compromise occurs — a used component is found compromised, a vendor is breached, a build pipeline is subverted — the organization must respond, and supply chain incidents have special challenges. Unlike a direct intrusion, a supply chain compromise means trusted software or a trusted vendor is the vector, so the malicious activity may look legitimate (it came through a trusted channel), the scope may be broad (a widely-used compromised component affects everything using it), and determining the impact requires knowing where the compromised element is used. The response applies incident response (BC-110) with these supply-chain-specific considerations: identifying the scope (which of your software/systems used the compromised component or vendor — where the SBOM is essential), containing (isolating affected systems, removing the compromised component, revoking compromised vendor access), and remediating (updating to a safe version, rebuilding, changing vendors).",
"SBOMs and visibility are decisive in supply chain incident response (the recurring value). When a component is found compromised (a malicious package, a backdoored dependency), the SBOM instantly identifies every affected application — turning the scoping (the hardest part of supply chain response) into an instant query, just as with vulnerability response (module ten). Without SBOMs and visibility, scoping a supply chain incident is a chaotic hunt (where is this compromised component used?), and the response is slow while the compromise persists. Supply chain incident response also involves coordinating with the source (the vendor or component maintainer) and, for widely-used compromises, the broader community (information sharing — BC-108). The special nature of supply chain incidents — trusted vectors, broad scope, and scoping-dependent-on-visibility — makes preparation (SBOMs, visibility, a supply-chain incident plan) essential to responding effectively. Understanding supply chain incident response — responding to compromises via trusted software or vendors, with SBOMs and visibility decisive for scoping — frames how the organization handles the incidents that the supply chain threat (module one) makes inevitable, applying incident response with the supply-chain-specific challenges and the visibility that makes response possible."],
ex:"A widely-used component is discovered to have been compromised (a backdoored version published). The organization responds: using its SBOMs, it instantly identifies every application containing the compromised version (scoping in minutes), contains them (isolating and removing the component), and remediates (updating to a safe version, checking for compromise). Organizations without SBOMs spend days hunting for where the component is used while the backdoor persists. The SBOMs and visibility made the supply-chain incident response fast and effective — decisive for scoping the trusted-vector compromise.",
refs:[["Supply chain incident response guidance","Responding to supply chain compromises."],["BC-110 incident response and module three/ten","IR and SBOM visibility."]],
kc:[
{q:"Supply chain incidents have the special challenge that:",o:["They are easy to scope","The vector is trusted software or a trusted vendor — malicious activity may look legitimate, and scope may be broad","They are always narrow","They are obvious"],a:1,x:"Trusted vectors make the activity look legitimate and the scope broad; scoping depends on knowing where the compromised element is used."},
{q:"SBOMs are decisive in supply chain incident response by:",o:["Blocking the attack","Instantly identifying every affected application when a component is found compromised — turning scoping into a query","Encrypting data","Nothing"],a:1,x:"The hardest part of supply-chain response is scoping (where is the compromised component used); the SBOM makes it an instant query."},
{q:"Preparation for supply chain incidents includes:",o:["Nothing","SBOMs, visibility, and a supply-chain incident plan — since scoping depends on visibility","Ignoring the supply chain","Trusting all software"],a:1,x:"Effective supply-chain incident response requires the SBOMs and visibility that make scoping possible — preparation is essential."}]},
{t:"Runtime Dependency Verification",d:"Confirming what actually runs.",
obj:["Explain runtime dependency verification","Describe the gap between declared and actual dependencies","Explain flagging and controlling runtime components"],
read:["Knowing your dependencies at build time (the SBOM, module four) is essential, but what actually runs can differ from what was declared — dependencies may be loaded dynamically, components may be added at runtime, containers may pull in unexpected elements, and the running system may not match the build-time inventory. Runtime dependency verification confirms what components are actually running in production, closing the gap between the declared/build-time picture and the runtime reality. This matters because a vulnerability or compromise in a component that is actually running is the real risk, and a component present at runtime but not in the build-time SBOM is a blind spot exactly where risk hides.",
"Runtime verification techniques include: monitoring what components and libraries are actually loaded and running (runtime application analysis), comparing the runtime components against the build-time SBOM (flagging discrepancies — components running that were not declared, or expected components missing), and controlling what can run (allowlisting, the application control from BC-103, applied to dependencies). This connects the build-time supply-chain visibility (SBOM) to the runtime reality, ensuring the organization knows and controls not just what it built but what is actually executing. The graded deliverable's mention of flagging runtime dependencies points to exactly this: verifying the actual runtime components against the expected ones. Runtime verification is especially important in dynamic environments (containers, cloud) where the runtime composition can diverge from the build. Understanding runtime dependency verification — confirming and controlling what components actually run, closing the build-time-versus-runtime gap — frames the operational completion of supply-chain visibility, ensuring the organization's knowledge and control of its components extends to what is actually executing in production, where the real risk lives."],
ex:"An organization verifies runtime dependencies: monitoring what libraries and components actually load in production and comparing them against the build-time SBOMs. This flags a container that pulled in an unexpected component at runtime not in the build-time inventory — a blind spot now surfaced and controlled. The runtime verification closed the gap between what was built and what actually runs, ensuring the supply-chain visibility extended to the executing reality where the real risk lives — exactly the runtime-dependency flagging the capstone framework provides.",
refs:[["Runtime application analysis and RASP","Verifying runtime components."],["BC-103 application control","Controlling what runs."]],
kc:[
{q:"Runtime dependency verification addresses:",o:["Only build-time","The gap between declared/build-time dependencies and what actually runs — dynamically loaded or unexpected runtime components","Nothing","Only source code"],a:1,x:"What runs can differ from what was built; runtime verification confirms the actual executing components where the real risk lives."},
{q:"A component running at runtime but not in the build-time SBOM is:",o:["Fine","A blind spot exactly where risk hides — surfaced by comparing runtime components against the SBOM","Impossible","Always safe"],a:1,x:"Undeclared runtime components are blind spots; runtime verification flags the discrepancy between expected and actual components."},
{q:"Runtime verification is especially important in:",o:["Static systems","Dynamic environments (containers, cloud) where runtime composition can diverge from the build","No environments","Only mainframes"],a:1,x:"Dynamic container/cloud environments can pull in unexpected runtime components, making runtime verification of the executing reality essential."}]},
{t:"Governance, Compliance, and SCRM Capstone",d:"Governing supply chain risk and running the program.",
obj:["Explain supply chain security governance and compliance","Describe regulatory drivers like EO 14028","Design a supply chain risk management program end to end"],
read:["Supply chain security requires governance and is increasingly driven by compliance. Governance establishes the program: policies for what components and vendors are acceptable, processes for assessment and monitoring, roles and accountability, and integration with the broader security program (the SR control family from BC-106, the C-SCRM of SP 800-161). Compliance is a major driver — US Executive Order 14028 and subsequent guidance require software producers selling to the government to provide SBOMs, attest to secure development (the SSDF from IC-203), and meet supply chain security requirements, and regulations increasingly mandate supply chain security across sectors. This regulatory push has made supply chain security a compliance obligation, not just a good practice, accelerating adoption of SBOMs, provenance, and vendor requirements.",
"The capstone designs a supply chain risk management program. It establishes visibility (SBOMs for what you build and consume — modules three, four), manages dependencies (controlled sources, vetting, monitoring — modules five, ten), secures the build and distribution (module six) with signing and provenance (module seven), manages vendor and hardware risk (modules eight, nine), continuously monitors and responds (module ten), and governs the whole with policy, accountability, and compliance (this module). The program addresses supply chain risk across its dimensions — components, build, distribution, vendors, hardware — continuously and under governance.",
"The graded deliverable — building a supply chain validation framework that ingests SBOMs, cross-references vulnerability feeds, and flags runtime dependencies — is exactly this: an operational capability for the visibility, monitoring, and response at the heart of supply chain risk management. Its quality is judged by whether it provides real supply-chain visibility and enables continuous monitoring and rapid response to component risk. An organization with a mature supply chain risk management program knows what is in its software and who it depends on, verifies integrity through the chain, and responds fast to the continuously-changing risk — managing the reality that its security depends on everything and everyone in its supply chain, the recognition this course exists to instill at the frontier of a threat that has reshaped security.",],
ex:"A team builds the capstone supply-chain validation framework: ingesting SBOMs for all software, cross-referencing components against vulnerability feeds continuously, and flagging runtime dependencies and their risk — providing real-time supply-chain visibility and response. Governed by policy (acceptable components/vendors), compliant with EO 14028 (SBOMs, secure-development attestation), and integrated with vendor and continuous-monitoring processes, the program manages supply chain risk across its dimensions. The framework provided the visibility and rapid response at the heart of supply-chain risk management — knowing what is in the software and responding to its changing risk.",
refs:[["US EO 14028 and NIST SBOM/SSDF guidance","Supply chain security compliance."],["NIST SP 800-161 and modules 2-11","The supply chain risk management program."]],
kc:[
{q:"US Executive Order 14028 drives supply chain security by:",o:["Banning software","Requiring software producers selling to the government to provide SBOMs, attest to secure development, and meet supply-chain requirements","Ignoring supply chain","Removing SBOMs"],a:1,x:"EO 14028 made SBOMs, secure-development attestation, and supply-chain security a compliance obligation, accelerating adoption."},
{q:"A supply chain risk management program addresses:",o:["Only your own code","Risk across its dimensions — components, build, distribution, vendors, hardware — continuously and under governance","Only vendors","Only hardware"],a:1,x:"The program spans components, build, distribution, vendors, and hardware, managed continuously and governed — the full supply-chain surface."},
{q:"A mature supply chain program's core capability is:",o:["Ignoring dependencies","Knowing what is in its software and who it depends on, verifying integrity, and responding fast to changing component risk","Trusting everything","One-time assessment"],a:1,x:"Visibility (SBOMs), integrity verification, and continuous response to changing risk are the heart of supply-chain risk management."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker compromises a widely-used software vendor's build pipeline and distributes malware as a trusted update to thousands. This is:",o:["A phishing attack","A supply-chain attack (SolarWinds-class) — exploiting trust for leverage across the downstream","A DoS","A password attack"],a:1,x:"Compromising the build pipeline flows malicious code to everyone downstream as trusted software — the leverage supply-chain attacks exploit."},
{q:"SCENARIO: When Log4Shell was disclosed, an organization instantly found every affected application. This was because it had:",o:["No dependencies","SBOMs — the component inventory turning a chaotic hunt into an instant targeted query","No software","Only its own code"],a:1,x:"SBOMs provide the visibility to instantly identify software containing an affected component — the transformative reactive value."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker publishes a public package matching a company's internal package name, hoping the build pulls it. This is and the defense is:",o:["Phishing; MFA","Dependency confusion; a controlled private registry that resolves internal names correctly and vets external packages","DoS; a firewall","Typosquatting; longer names"],a:1,x:"Dependency confusion exploits public-over-private resolution; a controlled dependency source resolving internal names correctly defeats it."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An organization signs its artifacts with SLSA provenance and consumers verify them. This counters:",o:["Slow builds","Supply-chain tampering and impersonation — tampered or illegitimate artifacts fail verification","Weak passwords","DoS"],a:1,x:"Signing and provenance let consumers verify authenticity and build integrity, countering the tampering supply-chain attacks rely on."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A vendor with deep access to an organization's systems is breached. Limited impact resulted because the vendor had:",o:["Unlimited access","Least-privilege access, limiting what the vendor (and thus the attacker) could reach","No access","Admin rights"],a:1,x:"Least-privilege vendor access limits the impact of a vendor compromise — the Target lesson of over-trusted vendor access."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A component safe last month has a critical vulnerability disclosed today. The organization responds instantly because it has:",o:["No monitoring","Continuous component monitoring with SBOMs — instantly flagging affected software for prioritized remediation","Static components","No SBOMs"],a:1,x:"Continuous monitoring against vulnerability feeds, using SBOMs, turns a newly-disclosed vulnerability into a managed instant response."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A developer discovers the application uses a vulnerable component as a deep transitive dependency they never knew about. This shows:",o:["Good visibility","The opacity of transitive dependencies — the deep tree hides components until one is vulnerable (Log4Shell), requiring SBOM visibility","No risk","Complete knowledge"],a:1,x:"Transitive dependencies are opaque; SBOMs provide the visibility into the deep tree where unknown risk hides."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A high-security organization worries about hardware implants. The management approach is:",o:["Trust all hardware","Procure from trusted vetted suppliers, verify firmware integrity, and control the hardware supply chain","Ignore hardware","Random procurement"],a:1,x:"Hardware provenance and assurance (trusted sources, firmware verification, controlled supply chain) manage the physical supply-chain risk."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A software producer must sell to the US government. EO 14028 requires it to:",o:["Nothing","Provide SBOMs, attest to secure development (SSDF), and meet supply-chain security requirements","Only encrypt","Remove all dependencies"],a:1,x:"EO 14028 made SBOMs, secure-development attestation, and supply-chain security a compliance obligation for government software."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A supply chain risk management program is judged mature. The correct standard is:",o:["It scans its own code","It knows what is in its software and who it depends on, verifies integrity through the chain, and responds fast to changing component risk","It ignores vendors","It is one-time"],a:1,x:"Visibility (SBOMs), integrity verification across the chain, and continuous response to changing risk define a mature supply-chain program."}
]};
